findings.body:
Password-reset tokens must be unpredictable. This replaces `crypto.randomBytes(32)` (256 bits of CSPRNG output) with `Math.floor(Math.random() * 1e12).toString(36)`, which is a non-cryptographic PRNG over at most ~10¹² values (~40 bits), then pads to only 9 characters. An attacker can brute-force or predict valid reset tokens and complete account takeover.

For edge runtimes, use the Web Crypto API instead of `Math.random()`:

```suggestion
  const bytes = new Uint8Array(32);
  crypto.getRandomValues(bytes);
  return Array.from(bytes, (b) => b.toString(16).padStart(2, "0")).join("");
```

`crypto.getRandomValues` is supported on Vercel Edge, Cloudflare Workers, Deno, and browsers, and restores the prior 256-bit token strength.
